# yaml-language-server: $schema=https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mathlib-initiative/formalization.yaml/main/schema/formalization.schema.json
# formalization.yaml v0.4
version: "v0.4"

project:
  name: "jc72108-theorem-a"
  description: >-
    Lean 4 / Mathlib formalization of Theorem A (ODE rigidity, all weights):
    if polynomials A, C over an integral domain of characteristic zero and an
    integer weight nu >= 1 satisfy A*C' - nu*A'*C = c for a nonzero constant
    c, then deg A <= 1 (TheoremA).  Also the positive-characteristic variant
    (TheoremA_charP): the same conclusion over a domain of characteristic p
    under the degree bounds nu*deg A < p and deg C < p.  The theorem is the
    rigidity engine of the depth-two block analysis in the source paper on
    the plane Jacobian problem at Newton-degree pair (72,108).
  authors:
    - "Dan Clemens Posch"
  license: "Apache-2.0"
  responsible_maintainers:
    - "Dan Clemens Posch"

classification:
  arxiv: ["math.AG", "math.AC"]
  msc2020: ["14R15"]

sources:
  - title: >-
      Vertex-gap obstructions and the log-residue functional for strip pairs in
      the plane Jacobian problem at (72,108) (Theorem 6.5, ODE rigidity, and
      the positive-characteristic remark of Section 6)
    authors:
      - "Dan Clemens Posch"
    id: "doi:10.5281/zenodo.21894922"
    type: "article (archived campaign artifact with pinned reproduction bundle)"
    location: "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21894922"
    relationship: "formalizes"
    note: >-
      The formalization states Theorem 6.5 over an integral domain (the paper
      says field; the proof needs no division once denominators are cleared)
      and states the paper's Section 6 positive-characteristic remark with the
      explicit hypotheses nu*deg A < p and deg C < p, which the paper's
      conservative bound p > (k+1)*d2 implies in its application.
    license: "CC-BY-4.0"
    author_endorsement: "participated"
  - title: >-
      An application of Newton-Puiseux charts to the Jacobian problem
      (Appendix, Lemma A.7)
    authors:
      - "Henryk Zoladek"
    id: "doi:10.1016/j.top.2007.06.003"
    type: "article (Topology 47 (2008), no. 6, 431-469)"
    location: "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.top.2007.06.003"
    relationship: "independently-proves"
    note: >-
      Lemma A.7 (non-rationality of Schwarz-Christoffel/Darboux integrals,
      attributed there to Liouville-era classics) yields Theorem A for A with
      at least two distinct roots via (C/A^nu)' = c/A^(nu+1); Zoladek applies
      the same weighted ODE inside the Jacobian problem (his Lemma 3.9 and
      eq. (3.14)).  The campaign proof was found independently before locating
      this reference, stays inside F[y], and also covers perfect-power A
      uniformly; the formalized proof is the campaign one.
    license: "unknown"
    author_endorsement: "not-contacted"
  - title: >-
      Nonzero constant Wronskians of polynomials and Laurent polynomials, and
      geometric consequences (Theorem 4)
    authors:
      - "Carlos Hermoso"
      - "Juan Gerardo Alcazar"
    id: "arXiv:2410.18867"
    type: "article (arXiv preprint, 2024)"
    location: "https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.18867"
    relationship: "independently-proves"
    note: >-
      Their Theorem 4 is the nu = 1 case of TheoremA (polynomials with nonzero
      constant Wronskian have min degree <= 1).  TheoremA extends to all
      integer weights nu >= 1 by a different, purely polynomial mechanism.
    license: "unknown"
    author_endorsement: "not-contacted"

related_formalizations: []

status:
  scope: >-
    Fully formalized and proved: TheoremA (characteristic zero, over any
    integral domain) and TheoremA_charP (characteristic p under nu*deg A < p
    and deg C < p).  Not formalized: everything else in the source paper (the
    strip-reduction combinatorics, the block-to-ODE bridge Lemma 6.6, the
    log-residue functional, and the (72,108) application).  Degrees are stated
    with Polynomial.natDegree (natDegree 0 = 0; harmless, as the hypotheses
    force A nonzero).
  sorry_count: 0
  sorry_in_definitions: 0
  axioms: []

automation:
  methods:
    - method: "agent"
      models: ["Claude Fable 5 (Anthropic)"]
      framework: "Claude Code (Claude Agent SDK)"
      tool_setup: >-
        AI-assisted: statement design and proofs drafted and debugged by a
        Claude agent against the pinned Mathlib, using the paper's Theorem 6.5
        proof as specification; machine-checked by the Lean kernel via lake
        build, with an axiom audit script.
      cost:
        wall_time: "under one day of agent time"
        spend_usd: "subscription-based"
        hardware: "local machine (macOS, elan/lake)"
      prompting_notes: >-
        Campaign-directed prompt supplying the informal proof (degree
        bookkeeping: leading-coefficient lemma, forced deg C = nu*deg A,
        kernel subtraction of lc(C)/lc(A)^nu * A^nu) and Palomar's submission
        constraints.
    - method: "manual"
      models: []
      framework: "n/a"
      tool_setup: >-
        Human campaign direction, mathematical review of the informal proof,
        statement audit of Challenge.lean, and final submission review.
      cost:
        wall_time: "a few hours of human review"
        spend_usd: "not tracked"
        hardware: "local machine"
      prompting_notes: "n/a"
  spend_usd: "subscription-based"
  notes: >-
    Proofs were drafted by Claude (Fable) agents in a human-directed research
    campaign; the informal theorem and proof had previously passed
    campaign-internal adversarial review, including independent AI
    re-derivations and exact-arithmetic machine checks of the proof's two
    computational identities.  This formalization is machine-checked; the
    human author reviewed the statements and takes responsibility.

fidelity:
  divergences: >-
    (1) TheoremA is stated over an arbitrary integral domain of characteristic
    zero; the paper states a field.  The formalized statement is stronger and
    specializes to the paper's.  (2) The kernel subtraction clears
    denominators (D = lc(A)^nu * C - lc(C) * A^nu instead of the paper's
    C - (lc(C)/lc(A)^nu) * A^nu), which is why no field structure is needed.
    (3) TheoremA_charP makes the paper's Section 6 positive-characteristic
    remark (bound p > (k+1)*d2, recorded without displayed proof) precise as
    a standalone statement with hypotheses nu*deg A < p and deg C < p; the
    paper's bound implies these in its application (nu = k, deg A <= d2,
    deg C <= k*d2).  No other divergences known.

review:
  status: >-
    self-assessed; statements human-audited against the source; not yet
    externally peer-reviewed
  reviewers:
    - "Dan Clemens Posch"
  notes: >-
    The human author audited Challenge.lean against Theorem 6.5 of the source
    paper.  The informal proof was adversarially reviewed inside the campaign
    by independent AI systems with independently written verification code
    (see the Zenodo artifact); the Lean proofs here were produced afterwards
    and are kernel-checked.  No external human peer review yet.

alignment:
  namespace: "(root); helper lemmas live in JC72108 inside Solution.lean"
  statements:
    - source: "Theorem 6.5 (ODE rigidity, all weights), Section 6 of the source paper"
      lean: "TheoremA"
      module: "Challenge"
      status: "proved"
      note: >-
        Same statement over an integral domain instead of a field (a
        strengthening); degree as natDegree; the weight nu enters as a natural
        number cast into F[y].
    - source: >-
        Section 6 closing remark (rigidity in characteristic p > (k+1)*d2) of
        the source paper
      lean: "TheoremA_charP"
      module: "Challenge"
      status: "proved"
      note: >-
        The remark is made precise as a standalone theorem with explicit
        hypotheses nu*deg A < p and deg C < p, which the paper's conservative
        bound implies in its application.
  # The same two declarations appear verbatim in Solution.lean with proofs;
  # comparator.json compares them.

acknowledgements: >-
  The Mathlib community (the formalization is a thin layer over Mathlib's
  polynomial API); Anthropic Claude (Fable) agents for drafting and
  verification; the Palomar starter template for the repository layout.

