     8	/-!
     9	# Challenge: rigidity of the weighted derivation identity (Theorem A)
    10	
    11	Statement module (Palomar registry).  The result:
    12	
    13	**Theorem A** (ODE rigidity, all weights).  Let `F` be an integral domain of
    14	characteristic zero, `ν ≥ 1` an integer, and let `A, C ∈ F[y]` satisfy
    15	
    16	  `A·C′ − ν·A′·C = c`  for some constant `c ∈ F`, `c ≠ 0`.
    17	
    18	Then `deg A ≤ 1`.
    19	
    20	This is Theorem 6.5 of the source paper (Zenodo, DOI 10.5281/zenodo.21894922),
    21	where it closes the depth-two block of the strip reduction of the plane
    22	Jacobian problem at Newton-degree pair `(72,108)`: through the block-to-ODE
    23	bridge it forces the edge polynomial `A` of a hypothetical Keller
    24	counterexample to be binomial.  Dividing the identity by `A^{ν+1}` gives
    25	`(C/A^ν)′ = c/A^{ν+1}`, so the statement is a non-rationality lemma for
    26	`∫ dy/A^{ν+1}`: it adapts / independently proves Lemma A.7 in the appendix of
    27	Żołądek, *An application of Newton–Puiseux charts to the Jacobian problem*,
    28	Topology 47 (2008) 431–469, and for `ν = 1` (nonzero constant Wronskian)
    29	Theorem 4 of Hermoso–Alcázar, arXiv:2410.18867.  The paper stated it over a
    30	field; an integral domain suffices and is what is formalized.
    31	
    32	`TheoremA_charP` is the positive-characteristic variant recorded in §6 of the
    33	paper: the same rigidity holds over a domain of characteristic `p` once `p`
    34	dominates the degree data, `ν·deg A < p` and `deg C < p`.  The paper's
    35	conservative application bound `p > (k+1)·d₂` (used there with `ν = k`,
    36	`deg A ≤ d₂`, `deg C ≤ k·d₂`) implies both hypotheses.  Some bound is
    37	necessary: in characteristic `p` the pair `A = y^p + y`, `C = 1`, `ν = 1` has
    38	`A·C′ − A′·C = −1` and `deg A = p ≥ 2`.
    39	
    40	Reading conventions (all standard Mathlib):
    41	* `Polynomial.derivative` is the formal derivative on `F[y]`.
    42	* `Polynomial.natDegree` is the degree as a natural number, with
    43	  `natDegree 0 = 0` — harmless here, since the hypotheses force `A ≠ 0`.
    44	* `Polynomial.C : F →+* F[y]` embeds constants, so `Polynomial.C c` is the
    45	  constant polynomial `c`, and `(ν : Polynomial F)` is the natural number `ν`
    46	  cast into `F[y]`.
    47	* `CharP F p` says `F` has characteristic `p`; an integral domain has
    48	  characteristic zero or a prime.
    49	-/
    50	
    51	/-- **Theorem A** (ODE rigidity, all weights; characteristic zero).
    52	If polynomials `A, C` over an integral domain `F` of characteristic zero
    53	satisfy `A·C′ − ν·A′·C = c` with integer weight `ν ≥ 1` and `c` a nonzero
    54	constant, then `deg A ≤ 1`.  (Theorem 6.5 of the source paper.) -/
    55	theorem TheoremA {F : Type*} [CommRing F] [IsDomain F] [CharZero F]
    56	    (ν : ℕ) (hν : 1 ≤ ν) (A C : Polynomial F) (c : F) (hc : c ≠ 0)
    57	    (hode : A * Polynomial.derivative C
    58	        - (ν : Polynomial F) * (Polynomial.derivative A * C) = Polynomial.C c) :
    59	    A.natDegree ≤ 1 := by
    60	  sorry
    61	
    62	/-- **Theorem A, positive characteristic.**  Over an integral domain of
    63	characteristic `p`, the same rigidity holds provided `p` dominates the degree
    64	data: `ν·deg A < p` and `deg C < p`.  (Recorded in §6 of the source paper via
    65	the conservative bound `p > (k+1)·d₂`, which implies both hypotheses in the
    66	application there.) -/
    67	theorem TheoremA_charP {F : Type*} [CommRing F] [IsDomain F] (p : ℕ) [CharP F p]
    68	    (ν : ℕ) (hν : 1 ≤ ν) (A C : Polynomial F) (c : F) (hc : c ≠ 0)
    69	    (hνA : ν * A.natDegree < p) (hC : C.natDegree < p)
    70	    (hode : A * Polynomial.derivative C
    71	        - (ν : Polynomial F) * (Polynomial.derivative A * C) = Polynomial.C c) :
    72	    A.natDegree ≤ 1 := by
    73	  sorry
